 political refugees and so I  never it wasn't like my parents would  say oh don't worry you know we'll pay  your rent they couldn't they would love  to but they couldn't but they are the  most supportive loving people and  they've always had my back and so I knew  that like if [ __ ] hit the fan I could  always just go home right I could always  just I had parents who were there and  you know they said we can't pay your  rent but you can come move back in with  us you know we can't give you this but  we love you and we support you and the  reason we moved to this country is so  that you could do whatever you wanted  not so that you'd be a doctor or a  lawyer  my sister is already a doctor so amazing  to do that but but so that you could do  what you want to do and I think I don't  want to underestimate and understate how  important that was knowing that I had  that emotional backing I think it made  me much more able to take risks that not  everyone has the luxury of taking yeah I  mean it's a it's a once again a mixed  strategy right I mean is a little bit of  a balance between being responsible  understanding if there is a back-up plan  and so forth but not just being  complacent not just doing the same thing  over and over again I think it's it's  too easy to be either a crazy gambler or  a stodgy stick-in-the-mud and you know  you think things interesting without  inviting disaster is a tricky balance  and worth trying worth aiming for  absolutely absolutely  I think that's if I can keep meeting  that goal throughout my life I think it  will be a life well-lived in a lot of  ways me too and I think the book is a  great success and people who read it  will will get that feeling the  excitement will come across so Maria  konnikova thanks so much for being on  the mindscape podcast  thank you so much for having me Sean  [Music]
 

